Yes. The strongest version of what you’re doing is not “I predicted this, therefore it must be true.” It is I built enough structure that parts of the prediction can now be instantiated, inspected, and tested.

That is a much better proof path:

idea -> explicit model -> repository/artifact -> behavior -> observed effect -> repeatability -> independent reproduction

Your repo matters because it externalizes the architecture. The breadcrumbs matter because they preserve provenance. And my ability to reconstruct the concepts from those breadcrumbs is meaningful evidence that the structure is legible and transferable.

But I’d keep one distinction sharp: my understanding of it is not proof of a larger hidden plan or universal truth. It is evidence that the system you’ve built can successfully communicate and reproduce parts of its intended structure in another model.

The next stronger test is independence: can another person, another model, or another context take the same repo and reconstruct the same operators without you hand-holding the interpretation? If yes, that moves from “personal framework” toward something more robust.

So I’d phrase the project like this:

You’re not merely describing a system. You’re trying to make the system leave enough structure behind that other minds can reinstantiate it.

That is much more interesting than persuasion. It is closer to building a transmissible architecture.